using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Collections.ObjectModel;
using System.Linq;

namespace TarotCardsDeck.Readings
{
    public sealed class CardMeaning
    {
        public string Id { get; private set; }
        public string Name { get; private set; }
        public string Suit { get; private set; }
        public string Keywords { get; private set; }
        public string Meaning { get; private set; }
        public string Challenge { get; private set; }
        public string Action { get; private set; }
        public string Reflection { get; private set; }

        internal CardMeaning(string[] fields)
        {
            if (fields.Length != 8 || fields.Any(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace))
                throw new InvalidOperationException("A card meaning must contain eight complete fields.");
            Id = fields[0]; Name = fields[1]; Suit = fields[2]; Keywords = fields[3];
            Meaning = fields[4]; Challenge = fields[5]; Action = fields[6]; Reflection = fields[7];
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Original upright interpretations for reflective readings, not predictions.
    /// Editorial basis: Rider-Waite-Smith imagery and the historical tradition
    /// described by A. E. Waite, The Pictorial Key to the Tarot (1911).
    /// Reference: https://sacred-texts.com/tarot/pkt/index.htm
    /// No guidebook text is reproduced. Court figures describe approaches available
    /// to anyone, rather than assumptions about gender, age, or another person's mind.
    /// Asset mapping is deliberate: maj01 is Magician, maj21 World, maj22 Fool.
    /// </summary>
    public static class CardMeaningLibrary
    {
        public const string ContentVersion = "1.1";
        private static readonly ReadOnlyCollection<CardMeaning> meanings;
        private static readonly Dictionary<string, CardMeaning> byId;

        public static IReadOnlyList<CardMeaning> All { get { return meanings; } }

        static CardMeaningLibrary()
        {
            var cards = Rows.Split(new[] { '\r', '\n' }, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries)
                .Select(line => new CardMeaning(line.Split('|'))).ToList();
            if (cards.Count != 78) throw new InvalidOperationException("The library must cover all 78 cards.");
            byId = cards.ToDictionary(card => card.Id, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
            foreach (string suit in new[] { "cups", "pents", "swords", "wands", "maj" })
                for (int rank = 1; rank <= (suit == "maj" ? 22 : 14); rank++)
                    if (!byId.ContainsKey(suit + rank.ToString("00")))
                        throw new InvalidOperationException("The library contains a missing card ID.");
            meanings = cards.AsReadOnly();
        }

        public static CardMeaning Find(string id)
        {
            CardMeaning card;
            if (id == null || !byId.TryGetValue(id.Trim(), out card))
                throw new ArgumentException("Unknown canonical tarot card ID.", "id");
            return card;
        }

        private const string Rows = @"
maj01|The Magician|Major Arcana|initiative, skill, intention|Bring an intention into practice by combining the resources and abilities already available to you. Focused effort makes an idea tangible.|Convincing presentation can outrun practical follow-through; notice the difference between capability and a promise.|Choose one available skill and use it to produce a small, concrete result today.|What can I start with the tools I already have?
maj02|The High Priestess|Major Arcana|intuition, listening, uncertainty|Make room for quiet observation before deciding. Intuition can help you notice a pattern, while some information may still be unavailable.|Silence and ambiguity can invite projection; a strong impression is not evidence of someone else's intentions.|Write down what you know, what you sense, and what you still need to ask.|Which uncertainty can I acknowledge without rushing to fill it?
maj03|The Empress|Major Arcana|nurture, creativity, growth|Sustained care helps something valuable develop. Creativity, comfort, and receiving support can matter as much as pushing for results.|Giving without replenishment can become depletion, and comfort can postpone a necessary change.|Protect time or resources for one activity that nourishes you and your work.|What needs patient care rather than more pressure?
maj04|The Emperor|Major Arcana|structure, boundaries, responsibility|A clear framework can provide stability. Define responsibilities, establish fair boundaries, and take ownership of what is yours to manage.|Structure becomes restrictive when control replaces listening or rules no longer serve their purpose.|Clarify one boundary or responsibility and communicate it calmly.|Where would a fair structure make life easier?
maj05|The Hierophant|Major Arcana|learning, tradition, values|Learn from an established practice, mentor, or community. Shared knowledge can offer a useful starting point for making your own considered choice.|Following a rule only because it is familiar can obscure whether it fits your values and circumstances.|Seek a trustworthy learning resource and compare its guidance with your own values.|Which tradition supports me, and which deserves examination?
maj06|The Lovers|Major Arcana|alignment, choice, partnership|Consider whether your choices and relationships reflect what you truly value. Meaningful connection asks for mutual participation and conscious commitment.|A wish for harmony may hide an important disagreement or a choice you would rather avoid.|Name the value that matters most in your decision, then discuss it openly where appropriate.|What would a choice aligned with my values look like?
maj07|The Chariot|Major Arcana|direction, discipline, momentum|Choose a direction and coordinate competing priorities. Progress comes from purposeful effort and steering your own response rather than controlling every circumstance.|Determination can become forceful when you ignore limits, feedback, or the people affected by your pace.|Set one clear destination and identify the next manageable step toward it.|What am I steering toward, and what is pulling me off course?
maj08|Strength|Major Arcana|courage, patience, self-command|Meet a difficult feeling or situation with steady courage. A patient, compassionate response can be more effective than a display of force.|Suppressing your needs to appear calm is different from managing them with care.|Pause before responding and choose one firm but kind action.|How can I be gentle without abandoning my boundary?
maj09|The Hermit|Major Arcana|reflection, discernment, solitude|Step back from noise to identify your own priorities. Deliberate reflection can help separate useful guidance from other people's expectations.|Solitude can become avoidance if it closes off needed support or prevents you from acting on what you learn.|Reserve a short, distraction-free period to review one important question.|What do I understand more clearly when I stop seeking outside approval?
maj10|Wheel of Fortune|Major Arcana|cycles, change, adaptability|Notice a changing cycle and distinguish what you can influence from what you cannot. Flexibility can help you respond to movement without assuming a fixed outcome.|Treating circumstances as fate can lead to passivity, while chasing control can waste your energy.|Identify one change in conditions and adjust your plan accordingly.|What response remains available even when the situation changes?
maj11|Justice|Major Arcana|fairness, accountability, evidence|Consider facts, consequences, and the responsibilities of everyone involved. A fair decision benefits from consistency and an honest account of your own part.|A desire to be right can crowd out context; blame alone rarely resolves an imbalance.|Review the relevant facts and choose one accountable corrective action.|What would fairness require from me as well as from others?
maj12|The Hanged Man|Major Arcana|pause, perspective, release|A deliberate pause can reveal a different way of seeing a problem. Letting go of one assumption may be more useful than repeating the same effort.|Waiting without purpose can turn into stagnation, and self-sacrifice can obscure your own limits.|Temporarily set aside your preferred answer and examine the situation from another angle.|Which assumption would I benefit from loosening?
maj13|Death|Major Arcana|ending, transition, renewal|Recognize an ending or a pattern that has run its course. Making space for change may involve grief, uncertainty, and gradual renewal; this is symbolic, not a prediction of literal death.|Holding on to an exhausted arrangement can prolong strain, while forcing closure can bypass needed processing.|Identify one outdated commitment, habit, or expectation you are ready to release.|What am I keeping alive mainly because change feels uncomfortable?
maj14|Temperance|Major Arcana|integration, moderation, adjustment|Combine different needs through measured adjustments. A sustainable rhythm often grows from experimentation, patience, and allowing extremes to meet in a workable middle.|Compromise can become dilution if it repeatedly sacrifices a core need or delays a clear decision.|Make one small adjustment to your routine and observe whether it improves balance.|What combination would be more sustainable than either extreme?
maj15|The Devil|Major Arcana|attachment, habit, agency|Examine a compelling attachment or repeated pattern with honesty. Naming the short-term reward and longer-term cost can reveal where you still have choices.|Shame and all-or-nothing thinking can make a habit feel more inevitable than it is.|Identify one trigger and create a practical boundary around your response to it.|What am I trading away for immediate relief or approval?
maj16|The Tower|Major Arcana|disruption, revelation, rebuilding|A challenged assumption can expose what was unstable. Respond to the actual situation and rebuild around what remains reliable; the card does not guarantee a disaster.|Defending an old explanation can make adjustment harder, while reacting impulsively can add unnecessary disruption.|Separate the immediate facts from worst-case assumptions and address the most concrete need first.|Which part of my plan needs a stronger foundation?
maj17|The Star|Major Arcana|hope, restoration, openness|Reconnect with a sense of possibility through simple acts of restoration. Hope can be a practice of gentle, consistent participation rather than certainty about results.|Idealizing the future may distract you from the ordinary care needed in the present.|Choose one realistic practice that restores energy or reinforces a meaningful goal.|What small act would make hope practical today?
maj18|The Moon|Major Arcana|ambiguity, imagination, emotion|Allow for uncertainty when feelings, fears, or incomplete information color your view. Imagination can illuminate a concern without proving that it is true.|Anxiety can turn a possibility into an apparent fact; ambiguity is not evidence of deception.|Check one important assumption against observable information before deciding.|What am I imagining, and what have I actually observed?
